#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89246796-f084-535b-9bcf-74cb453b66b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(64x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291737} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac8127-e14e-56c4-a515-c5897606da56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291738} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0987-5dd6-58f6-afd4-e850f1bd4974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291739} {:answer/id #uuid "a256eb39-0a5c-5bdf-b751-4a56f59a8ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291740} {:answer/id #uuid "a42e9ab2-f8e1-5790-8b1e-01e07d816281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0987-5dd6-58f6-afd4-e850f1bd4974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291739} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69123c2-7d5f-5bb8-933f-37f2977e820c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c758c6a-6697-5dfb-b64a-bc653c6c226b" :question/math-academy-id "q-69476" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=8\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $8x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=8x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8\\int \\frac{1}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =8\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
